.. Template — reStructuredText tables.
..
.. Copy the form you need and replace the placeholder text. The decision rule
.. is at the bottom: read it before picking a form.
..
.. This template is deliberately generic: it describes reStructuredText and
.. Sphinx, not any one project. A project that wants a single mandatory form
.. states that in its own conventions and points here for the syntax.


Form 1 — ``list-table`` (default; pick this unless you have a reason not to)
============================================================================

Each row is a bullet; each cell is a nested bullet. Nothing has to line up, so
the table survives editing, wide cells, code spans and multi-line prose.

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 20 30 50

   * - Column A
     - Column B
     - Column C
   * - ``short``
     - a cell that happens to be longer
     - a cell whose prose wraps across several lines without breaking the
       table, because nothing in ``list-table`` depends on column alignment
   * - ``another``
     - —
     - use ``—`` for an empty cell; a bare ``-`` starts a new bullet

Options worth knowing:

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 22 78

   * - Option
     - What it does
   * - ``:header-rows: 1``
     - first row renders as ``<th>``. Omit it and every row is a body row
   * - ``:widths: 20 30 50``
     - relative column widths. Without it the builder guesses, and wide code
       spans push the layout around
   * - ``:widths: auto``
     - let the writer/builder decide — acceptable when columns are even
   * - ``:stub-columns: 1``
     - first *column* also renders as a header
   * - ``:align: center``
     - table alignment on the page
   * - ``:class: longtable``
     - LaTeX: allow the table to break across pages


Form 2 — grid table
====================

Explicit borders. The only form that supports **spanning cells**, which is its
one real reason to exist. Every edit means redrawing the borders by hand.

.. table:: Optional caption
   :widths: auto

   +--------------+------------------+-------------------+
   | Column A     | Column B         | Column C          |
   +==============+==================+===================+
   | cell         | cell             | cell              |
   +--------------+------------------+-------------------+
   | this cell spans two columns     | cell              |
   +--------------+------------------+-------------------+

A spanning cell drops the internal ``|``, and its text must fit the **summed**
width of the columns it covers plus the separator it swallowed. Getting that
off by one character is a build error, and it is the reason this form is the
last resort: the alignment is arithmetic you have to redo on every edit. If you
write one, compute the widths — do not eyeball them.


Form 3 — simple table
======================

Underline-delimited. Compact to read in raw form, but the column boundaries
are positional: a cell that grows past its column silently corrupts the table,
and cells cannot contain blank lines.

==========  ==========  ==========
Column A    Column B    Column C
==========  ==========  ==========
cell        cell        cell
cell        cell        cell
==========  ==========  ==========


Form 4 — ``csv-table``
=======================

For data that already exists as CSV, or is generated. Do not hand-write a
``csv-table`` you could have written as a ``list-table``: quoting rules make it
harder to edit, not easier.

.. csv-table:: Optional caption
   :header: "Column A", "Column B"
   :widths: 30, 70

   "value", "value"
   "value with, a comma", "value"

From a file — shown as source, because the directive resolves the path at build
time and a template has no data file to point at:

.. code-block:: rst

   .. csv-table:: From a file
      :file: data/measurements.csv
      :header-rows: 1


Decision rule
=============

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 30 70

   * - Situation
     - Form
   * - Anything, by default
     - ``list-table``
   * - A cell must span rows or columns
     - grid table — the only form that can
   * - The data is already a CSV file, or generated
     - ``csv-table`` with ``:file:``
   * - A tiny fixed table you will never edit again
     - simple table is *acceptable*, not better

Why ``list-table`` is the default, stated as consequences rather than taste:

- **It does not break when edited.** Simple and grid tables encode column
  boundaries in whitespace; widening one cell silently misaligns the row. A
  malformed table is not always a build error — it can render as wrong content.
- **It holds arbitrary cell content.** Multi-line prose, nested lists,
  ``code-block``, admonitions. Simple tables cannot contain blank lines at all.
- **Column widths are declared, not inferred.** ``:widths:`` states intent;
  the other forms make the builder guess from character counts.
- **Diffs stay legible.** Changing one cell changes one line. In a simple or
  grid table it rewrites the borders too, so review cannot see what changed.

The cost is real and worth naming: ``list-table`` is verbose, and the raw
source is harder to read than a simple table. That cost is paid once at
writing; the alignment cost of the other forms is paid at every edit.


Checks before committing a table
=================================

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 40 60

   * - Check
     - How
   * - The table parses
     - build the file, or parse it standalone with ``docutils``
   * - Every row has the same number of cells
     - ``list-table`` errors loudly on this; the other forms may not
   * - ``:widths:`` has one number per column
     - a mismatch is a build error, and it is easy to miss after adding a
       column
   * - The header row is marked
     - ``:header-rows: 1`` — a table whose first row is body text reads as
       data, not as a heading
